{:edb/db-before-t 1229 :edb/db-after-t 1230 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534542 50 #inst "2026-10-07T21:47:56.190Z" 13194139534542 true] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293911 13194139534542 true] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293918 13194139534542 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293890 13194139534542 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293897 13194139534542 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186293904 13194139534542 true] [17592186293890 1017 #edb/ref 17592186293891 13194139534542 true] [17592186293890 1089 "q-160619" 13194139534542 true] [17592186293890 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534542 true] [17592186293890 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/f3e31d1c15a97adea32790eafe75ad1bc9f70c854b516b98de6648ec8d7bcb87.png)\n\nConsider the vector $v$ shown above. Find the vector $Av$ given that \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139534542 true] [17592186293890 1113 #uuid "75802105-915e-5223-a4a4-b4c2831bb162" 13194139534542 true] [17592186293890 1145 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033087/assets/9fae3ec0ada3008937d88e4e76374a56da4369b1821cfee072c90d88b562df46.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need